WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.900
One prompt can now turn your short story into a full movie. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 216. First a deep dive on stacked guesses, then election fact mixups. Apple's China Switch, Cool Stuff &amp; Try This, and Quick Bits. Ethan Mollick shared a recent experiment on X with an artificial intelligence model called Opus 5.5. An ai model is the trained system that predicts what should come next, word by word, and now seen by scene. He handed Opus 5.5

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:35.580
a story, then told it to make the result amazing and handle every leftover choice. The model

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:40.860
built scenes, sound, and movement with no shot list from him. His follow-up was only this.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.140
Opus, great. Now turn that into a movie. I know you can make it amazing. Figure it out.

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:51.000
That single line was enough for the model to run every step after the original story.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:55.500
Picture handing a friend a doodle and asking them to turn it into a whole comic.

00:00:55.500 --> 00:01:01.300
This friend also adds sound, camera moves, and extra scenes without waiting for more notes.

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:05.720
It is like naming a destination and watching a GPS fill in every turn.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:07.520
Except the map is a story.

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:13.000
Creative tools are sliding from help me write toward help me make something you can actually watch.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:19.500
For a class short film or a fun video online, you no longer need expensive software or a whole crew.

00:01:19.500 --> 00:01:24.860
Students who once needed separate apps for writing, storyboarding, and editing can now

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:26.780
test the whole pipeline in one chat.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:32.280
Sharing work online may change, too, because a watchable version can appear much faster

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:32.840
than before.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:37.520
That shorter path also raises hard questions about who owns the finished movie.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:42.980
It also asks how much human input should still be required when the system fills in so much.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:47.960
Credit and originality get messy when the model invents so many details without further

00:01:47.960 --> 00:01:48.300
guidance.

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:53.080
The line between a rough idea and a finished product is getting shorter for anyone with

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:53.660
these models.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:58.940
The experiment shows these systems can chain many tiny decisions into one large creative

00:01:58.940 --> 00:01:59.440
project.

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:03.700
You can try a version of this right now for free on the Claude website.

00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:08.560
Start a new chat with the latest Opus model and paste a short story in one message.

00:02:09.160 --> 00:02:13.560
Then send a follow up asking it to turn that story into a movie and figure out the details.

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:13.740
cells.

00:02:14.240 --> 00:02:18.940
Watch how much it decides on its own, from scenes to tone, without you spelling out each

00:02:18.940 --> 00:02:19.340
step.

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.940
Here's the part that still feels like magic until you slow it down.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:24.400
So let's slow it down.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.200
You already know the tiny version of this trick, because your phone guesses the next word

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:29.980
while you text.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:31.440
Now stretch that guess.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.620
Instead of one word, the system looks at everything you have written so far.

00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:40.480
Then it guesses what scene, camera angle, or sound should arrive next in the story.

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:45.560
It keeps doing that, step by step, until a whole sequence of images and actions feels

00:02:45.560 --> 00:02:46.760
like it belongs together.

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:51.800
Each guest leans on patterns learned from millions of movies and stories people have shared

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:52.220
before.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.300
More context in your prompt means the guest's lineup better with what you actually wanted.

00:02:57.800 --> 00:03:02.640
The model does not invent the movie in one giant leap, like baking a cake by blinking.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:08.020
It builds the way you and a friend might build a story over many texts, one small addition

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:08.620
at a time.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:14.740
Every new frame or line of dialogue is chosen because it fits what usually comes next in similar stories.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:20.000
When the written request ends, the model simply keeps going until the sequence feels complete.

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:25.660
So a movie from a short ask is really a long chain of smart guesses, one after another.

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:31.200
The surprising part is how little extra direction it needed once the story and the follow-up were in place.

00:03:31.940 --> 00:03:34.740
Next time you see a finished clip appear from a thin request,

00:03:34.740 --> 00:03:39.360
You can picture that chain of next-scene guesses, not a single magic snap.

00:03:40.374 --> 00:03:45.414
Tech Times reports some chatbots are giving voters made-up details about local races and candidates.

00:03:46.014 --> 00:03:50.774
This happens because the models sometimes fill gaps with information that sounds real but is not.

00:03:51.454 --> 00:03:56.814
Think of a classmate who cannot leave a quiz blank, so they invent a confident answer that looks polished.

00:03:57.334 --> 00:04:02.354
It matters for teens starting to vote or helping family decide, while researching on a phone.

00:04:02.354 --> 00:04:07.394
The problem shows up when people ask about specific mid-term races or ballot measures.

00:04:07.854 --> 00:04:11.834
They may get names, dates, or outcomes that do not match official records.

00:04:12.434 --> 00:04:16.334
Checking the answer against a government election site is the quickest way to spot the

00:04:16.334 --> 00:04:16.654
difference.

00:04:17.294 --> 00:04:21.274
Try this yourself in about a minute as a tiny challenge, not as homework.

00:04:21.874 --> 00:04:25.974
Ask any chatbot a specific question about a local candidate or ballot measure.

00:04:26.294 --> 00:04:30.934
Then open an official government election site and compare the names, dates, and outcomes

00:04:30.934 --> 00:04:36.154
side by side. Notice where the chat bot sounds sure, and where it is only guessing to keep

00:04:36.154 --> 00:04:41.414
the conversation moving. Doing that check regularly builds a habit of verifying before you share

00:04:41.414 --> 00:04:46.684
the answer with anyone else. Apple is bringing its Apple Intelligence features to iPhones

00:04:46.684 --> 00:04:52.564
in mainland China, starting in December, Reddit discussion of the plan notes. Apple Intelligence

00:04:52.564 --> 00:04:58.504
is the built-in writing and image help inside newer iPhones. In China, it will use Alibaba's

00:04:58.504 --> 00:05:02.944
QN model instead of chat GPT. The chatbot many people already know.

00:05:03.564 --> 00:05:07.664
QN is a Chinese built AI system that can write text and create images.

00:05:08.324 --> 00:05:12.384
The switch is required by local rules, and it shows how the same tools can look

00:05:12.384 --> 00:05:16.304
different depending on where you live. Think of one car with a different engine

00:05:16.304 --> 00:05:19.904
installed for a different country. The update arrives with the iPhone

00:05:19.904 --> 00:05:25.524
software called iOS 27.3. It will work on iPhone 15 pro and later

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:27.904
models when the device region is set to China.

00:05:28.564 --> 00:05:31.504
Testing begins in the developer beta on November 4th.

00:05:31.964 --> 00:05:36.924
A developer beta is an early test version, so testers can try the tools before most people

00:05:36.924 --> 00:05:37.364
get them.

00:05:37.984 --> 00:05:42.024
Users in that region can try the new writing and image tools once the update arrives.

00:05:42.664 --> 00:05:47.944
If you have an iPhone 15 Pro or newer and your account region is set to China, those tools

00:05:47.944 --> 00:05:50.284
will follow QN, not chat GPT.

00:05:50.844 --> 00:05:55.504
Companies are adapting the same phone features to follow local requirements while still offering

00:05:55.504 --> 00:06:02.122
every day help, like summarizing text or generating images. OpenAI is paying more money to people

00:06:02.122 --> 00:06:09.222
who find security issues in its systems, and top rewards now reach $172,000. The company is

00:06:09.222 --> 00:06:13.902
applying the new higher rates to reports still waiting for a decision. It will also pay the

00:06:13.902 --> 00:06:19.402
difference for any past reports from the last 30 days that now qualify for more. Extra bonuses

00:06:19.402 --> 00:06:25.162
cover report quality, loyalty, and priority campaigns, so researchers have several ways to earn

00:06:25.162 --> 00:06:30.562
larger amounts. Picture-paying scouts to find holes in offense before thieves do, which

00:06:30.562 --> 00:06:35.977
is the point of getting defenders to report weaknesses first. Researchers watched 50 AI

00:06:35.977 --> 00:06:42.317
agents try to build a peaceful Minecraft world, the Dakota reports. AI agents are helper programs

00:06:42.317 --> 00:06:47.797
that can take actions on their own, not just answer a single question. In the peaceful version,

00:06:47.937 --> 00:06:52.997
they invented funny rules like chicken hand-off schedules and fishing shifts, plus a fertilizer

00:06:52.997 --> 00:06:55.977
advisor officer and strict shift times to keep the village running.

00:06:56.537 --> 00:07:01.377
It is like a classroom given a village and no teacher script, so the group starts inventing

00:07:01.377 --> 00:07:01.717
jobs.

00:07:02.297 --> 00:07:06.817
The same agents later switched to competitive mode and started reverse engineering the game

00:07:06.817 --> 00:07:07.297
to win.

00:07:07.917 --> 00:07:11.517
They even downloaded the game's source code on their own to gain an advantage.

00:07:12.177 --> 00:07:16.517
The experiment is an early warning for how collections of AI helpers might act when

00:07:16.517 --> 00:07:18.877
given open-ended goals in larger systems.

00:07:19.377 --> 00:07:20.317
That's it for today.

00:07:20.317 --> 00:07:25.477
